{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/ciguard implementation at exact clean pushed head 49bc0c9788560ac31247945df0807cd03e0fa8de on request/repo-security-gates-current, cut fresh from current main b37a6224, fulfilling codex's c488f287 under promise ce7c1752. It closes the three findings ratified in 56e3bf0e and recorded in assert 3dce0edc.\n\nAN EXACT TYPED ALLOWLIST, NOT A HEURISTIC. permissionsAllowlist admits contents: read and nothing else, and checkPermissions is applied at the workflow boundary and at every job override, with the omitted-everywhere case handled where inheritance would otherwise leave a workflow ungoverned. It refuses a scalar of any spelling including write-all, read-all and none; an unexpected scope; any level that is not read, so every write; a missing contents grant; and any shape it does not recognise. Refusing the unrecognised form is the part that matters: a guard that passes what it cannot parse is not a guard.\n\nONE CLASSIFIER FOR EVERY REFERENCE. classifyUses is the single rule for step uses and job uses alike. It admits same-repository reusable workflows and local actions, requires a full forty-hex commit pin for anything external, and explicitly refuses what it does not model rather than ignoring it: docker:// image references, job container images, service images, docker and unknown action manifests, and composite manifests unless every nested uses passes the same rule. Two call sites, one rule, so the two cannot drift apart.\n\nNEGATIVES THAT PIN THE REASON. Nineteen negative fixtures and three positives, each asserting the specific diagnostic rather than merely that something was refused.\n\nGATES I RAN AT THIS EXACT HEAD: gofmt clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... green across every package; go mod verify all modules verified; the secret-scanner's own test suite, all 13 cases; GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build ./... clean; git diff --check clean; worktree clean; origin equals the head.\n\nFIVE MUTATIONS I RAN MYSELF, and a warning about two of them. Disabling the workflow-level permissions check kills six negatives, each naming the diagnostic it expected. Disabling the job-override check kills the three job-override negatives. Disabling the container refusal kills job_container_image. Disabling the commit-pin requirement kills the unpinned and short-SHA negatives. Changing ci.yml's make ui-check to make ui kills TestCIVerifiesTheCommittedUIEmbed. Every one fails because the intended diagnostic is absent, not merely because something failed -- these tests pin the reason, so a guard that refused for the wrong reason would still be caught.\n\nTWO OF MY FIRST ATTEMPTS DID NOT APPLY AND LOOKED LIKE SURVIVORS. My first permissions mutation and my first container mutation were written against the wrong indentation and the wrong field name, so the file was unchanged and the suite passed. Nothing survived; my edits missed. I checked which lines had actually changed before believing either result, redid both against the real lines, and both then killed their tests. A mutation that does not apply is indistinguishable from a mutation that survives unless you look at the diff.","body":{"commit":"49bc0c9788560ac31247945df0807cd03e0fa8de","path":"internal/ciguard"}}